  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 138 PM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery with 500mb heights plotted show upper-level \nridging building into the middle of the country, with deep troughing \nover the eastern US and a strong upper low off the coast of the \nPacific Northwest. At the surface, an elongated high pressure area is \nspread from the Upper Midwest through the ArkLaTex, with the surface \nridge moving through the middle of the forecast area. As this high \npressure remains generally in place through tomorrow morning, \novernight lows will fall to their coldest levels of the season. Lows \ntonight should range from the lower 20s in central and northeastern \nMissouri, to the middle 20s through the KC area. With light winds \nexpected, a dry airmass, and no cloud cover expected, there may be \nsome sheltered areas that fall into the teens tomorrow morning. This \nis about 10 degrees below normal for this time of year and if some \nsheltered areas fall into the teens, it's closer to 15 degrees below \nnormal. That high pressure area slowly shifts to the east, \nwhich allows for a slightly greater pressure gradient and winds\nbecoming light from the east to southeast. That should help \ntemperatures to be not as cold Sunday morning vs Saturday \nmorning but we'll still see morning lows ranging from the lower \n20s to near freezing in the KC urban core. A similar moderation \nof overnight lows continues Monday morning but most areas are \nlikely to fall into the 20s, with the urban areas of KC near to \nslightly above freezing. By Tuesday, the surface high has \nshifted well east of the area and the upper ridging builds \neastward. This will lead surface winds to become southerly, with\nincreasing heights and thicknesses, allowing temperatures to \nrebound back to above normal levels. Normal temperatures for the\nmiddle of next week are in the lower 50s for highs and lower \n30s for lows and we should see highs climb into the 60s by\nWednesday and Thursday. By Friday, some our southeastern zones \nmay see highs climb into the 70s. \n\nFor the later half of next week, the persistent southerly low-level \nflow will increase moisture into the area. With the upper ridge \nshifting to the east broad troughing builds over the western half of \nthe US. This pattern leads to increasing precipitation chances \nacross the middle of country and we see lower chance probabilities \nof precipitation (15-30%) move into eastern KS and MO Thursday into \nFriday.",
